WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.960
Detect, Respond,

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:06.180
and Recover help organizations discover and manage cybersecurity incidents.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.920
Detect focuses on finding and analyzing possible attacks, anomalies, compromises,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.780
failures, and suspicious activity.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.400
Useful detection depends on logging, monitoring, alert logic, telemetry, ownership,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.580
and review.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.080
A detection outcome should be tied to the environment and risk,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.860
not copied blindly from another organization.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.520
Respond covers the actions taken during a cybersecurity event.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:38.900
That includes analysis, triage, containment, mitigation, communication, coordination,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.800
and incident management.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.220
Response is more effective when roles are clear before the incident.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.680
Teams should know who owns technical investigation, who communicates with leadership,

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.860
who coordinates with suppliers, who preserves evidence,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.800
and who approves major operational decisions.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.860
Recover focuses on restoring affected assets and operations.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:04.280
Recovery may involve backups, rebuilds, service restoration, validation,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.300
customer communication, and lessons learned.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.720
Recover is not simply the last step after an incident.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:16.380
It requires preparation: backup strategy, recovery objectives, dependency mapping,

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.000
restoration testing, and clear ownership.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:22.740
A recovery plan that has never been tested is still an assumption.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.240
These Functions require planning before the incident happens.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.700
Detection logic, response plans, communication paths, evidence handling,

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.160
decision authority, and recovery testing should be practiced and improved.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.940
CSF 2.0 helps teams discuss these outcomes in a structured way,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.560
but it does not guarantee incident readiness.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.780
Readiness comes from implemented processes, trained roles, useful evidence,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.680
and repeated validation.
